Q: What is the vanishing gradient problem?
A: This vanishing gradient problem is a big part of why deep networks were considered untrainable before about 2010. Three are cheap and revealing: Gradient norms per layer.
   sources: Chapter 4, "Vanishing gradients"; Chapter 14, "Watching training from the inside"  (match 4.24)

Q: Why use a validation set?
A: We use it to compare architectures, choose learning rates and decide when to stop training. Checking test accuracy after every experiment, and keeping whatever does best, quietly turns the test set into a second validation set.
   sources: Chapter 9, "Three datasets, three jobs"  (match 3.47)

